
<% content_for :title, "10 / 10 / 10 | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="ten-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="ten-hero">

    <picture class="ten-hero-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_ten_ten_ten_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_ten_ten_ten_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="ten-hero-content">

      <p class="ten-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / 10 / 10 / 10
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>10 /</span>
        <span>10 /</span>
        <span>10.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="ten-tagline">
        FUTURE YOU<br>
        GETS A VOTE.
      </p>

      <p class="ten-intro">
        ONE DECISION. THREE TIMEFRAMES.
        MORE THAN JUST RIGHT NOW.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE DECISION -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-black">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">01 / THE DECISION</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S THE<br>
        FUCKING DECISION?
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          You have a decision to make.
          Maybe it's buying something,
          sending a message, accepting
          a commitment, or doing something
          that could affect your sobriety.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whatever it is, you're probably
          paying the most attention to
          how you'll feel immediately
          afterward.
        </p>

        <p>
          But a decision can look very
          different depending on when
          you consider its consequences.
        </p>

        <p>
          10 / 10 / 10 asks you to
          consider how a choice might
          affect you in ten minutes,
          ten months, and ten years.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're not predicting
          the future. You're
          considering possibilities,
          consequences, and what
          matters to you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start by identifying
          exactly what you're
          deciding. Be specific.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Consider both options:
            what happens if you
            do it, and what happens
            if you don't?
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="ten-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT DECISION ARE YOU MAKING?
          WHAT ARE YOUR ACTUAL OPTIONS?
          DOES IT NEED TO BE DECIDED TODAY?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / TEN MINUTES -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-pink">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">02 / TEN MINUTES</p>

      <h2>
        TEN MINUTES<br>
        FROM NOW.
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          Start with the immediate
          consequences. What do you
          expect to experience
          shortly after making
          this decision?
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you'll feel relieved,
          excited, satisfied, anxious,
          disappointed, or uncomfortable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Be realistic about the
          immediate appeal. If buying
          something sounds exciting,
          acknowledge that. If sending
          a message would feel
          satisfying, acknowledge that.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to pretend
          an action has no benefits
          simply because it might
          have consequences later.
        </p>

        <p>
          But remember that the
          first ten minutes are
          only one part of
          the decision.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="ten-grid">

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DO IT.</h3>

          <p>
            What do you expect
            to gain immediately?
          </p>

          <p>
            Will you feel relief,
            pleasure, excitement,
            or satisfaction?
          </p>

          <p>
            Will anything become
            easier right away?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: You make an
            impulse purchase and
            enjoy the excitement
            of buying something
            you really want.
          </p>

          <p>
            What happens once
            that initial feeling
            begins to fade?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DON'T.</h3>

          <p>
            What will you experience
            if you choose not
            to act right now?
          </p>

          <p>
            Will you feel frustrated,
            disappointed, relieved,
            or uncertain?
          </p>

          <p>
            Is the discomfort
            temporary and manageable?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: You don't
            buy the item immediately.
            You might feel disappointed,
            but you still have
            your money and the
            option to reconsider.
          </p>

          <p>
            What are you giving up,
            and what are you
            protecting?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / TEN MONTHS -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-purple">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">03 / TEN MONTHS</p>

      <h2>
        TEN MONTHS<br>
        FROM NOW.
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          Now move beyond the
          immediate feeling.
          What might the consequences
          look like after enough
          time has passed for
          them to develop?
        </p>

        <p>
          Will the decision still
          matter? Will it have
          created opportunities,
          responsibilities, financial
          consequences, or changes
          in your relationships?
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about what you
          know from experience.
          What tends to happen
          after you make similar
          decisions?
        </p>

        <p>
          Some choices that feel
          wonderful initially
          become problems later.
          Others require discomfort
          now but produce benefits
          over time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider both without
          assuming either outcome
          is guaranteed.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="ten-grid">

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DO IT.</h3>

          <p>
            Will you still be
            satisfied with
            the decision?
          </p>

          <p>
            What responsibilities
            or consequences might
            remain?
          </p>

          <p>
            Has the decision
            affected your finances,
            relationships, health,
            or sobriety?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: A purchase
            might still be useful,
            or you might regret
            spending money on
            something you
            barely use.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does your
            previous experience
            suggest?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DON'T.</h3>

          <p>
            Will you still care
            that you passed
            up the opportunity?
          </p>

          <p>
            Will you be glad
            you waited, or
            might you regret
            not taking action?
          </p>

          <p>
            Did not acting
            protect something
            important?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: You might
            be pleased that
            you saved your
            money, or discover
            that the purchase
            was worth planning
            and budgeting for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that
            doing nothing can
            also have consequences.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / TEN YEARS -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-yellow">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">04 / TEN YEARS</p>

      <h2>
        TEN YEARS<br>
        FROM NOW.
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          Ten years is a long time.
          You have no way of
          knowing exactly what
          your life will look
          like by then.
        </p>

        <p>
          But you can consider
          whether the decision
          supports the kind
          of life you want.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some decisions will
          barely matter in
          ten years. Recognizing
          that can help you
          stop treating them
          like enormous events.
        </p>

        <p>
          Others can influence
          your health, finances,
          relationships, independence,
          or long-term stability.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about patterns,
          not just isolated events.
          What happens when you
          repeatedly make similar
          choices over time?
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need
          a perfect future.
          You need to consider
          whether today's decision
          is consistent with
          your broader priorities.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="ten-grid">

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DO IT.</h3>

          <p>
            Does this choice
            contribute to the
            life you want?
          </p>

          <p>
            Will its consequences
            still matter years
            from now?
          </p>

          <p>
            Is it part of
            a pattern you'd
            like to continue?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: Choosing
            to drink might offer
            immediate pleasure,
            but returning to
            that pattern could
            threaten the sobriety
            and stability you've
            worked to establish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider the real
            stakes without
            assuming the worst
            outcome is inevitable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ten-box">
          <h3>IF YOU DON'T.</h3>

          <p>
            What might you
            preserve by
            declining or waiting?
          </p>

          <p>
            Would you be protecting
            your health, finances,
            sobriety, or important
            relationships?
          </p>

          <p>
            Or are you avoiding
            something meaningful
            because you're afraid
            of uncertainty?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: Choosing
            not to drink protects
            your sobriety today
            and supports a
            long-term pattern
            you want to maintain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about what
            you want more of
            in your life,
            not only what
            you want to avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-black">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">05 / PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        YOU'RE NOT<br>
        A FORTUNE<br>
        TELLER.
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          You've considered
          ten minutes, ten months,
          and ten years.
          Now remember that
          none of those outcomes
          is guaranteed.
        </p>

        <p>
          This exercise isn't
          about predicting
          your future with
          perfect accuracy.
          It's about making
          room for consequences
          you might otherwise
          overlook.
        </p>

        <p>
          Don't turn it into
          another opportunity
          to catastrophize.
          A possible negative
          outcome isn't proof
          that everything
          will go wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          And don't assume
          something will work
          out beautifully
          simply because
          you're excited
          or confident.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your mood matters.
          Depression can make
          the future look hopeless.
          Mania can make ambitious
          plans seem unquestionably
          successful.
        </p>


        <p>
          Use actual information:
          your finances, commitments,
          previous experiences,
          priorities, and
          realistic consequences.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            YOU DON'T NEED TO
            KNOW WHAT WILL HAPPEN.
            YOU NEED TO CONSIDER
            WHAT COULD HAPPEN.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="ten-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT DO YOU KNOW?
          WHAT ARE YOU ASSUMING?
          WHICH CONSEQUENCES ARE
          REALISTIC, AND WHICH
          ARE JUST POSSIBILITIES?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE -->
  <section class="ten-section ten-pink">
    <div class="ten-section-inner">

      <p class="ten-number">06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT GETS<br>
        YOUR VOTE?
      </h2>

      <div class="ten-copy">

        <p>
          Now look at the
          decision as a whole.
        </p>

        <p>
          What benefits
          are you hoping for?
          What consequences
          are you willing
          to accept?
          What matters most
          to you?
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          automatically choose
          the option with
          the least immediate
          pleasure or the
          smallest amount of risk.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes a difficult
          decision now creates
          a worthwhile benefit
          later. Sometimes
          enjoying something
          in the present
          is perfectly reasonable.
        </p>

        <p>
          The point is to
          consider more than
          one timeframe before
          making the choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you need more
          information, get it.
          If you need another
          perspective, ask.
          If you aren't ready
          to decide, consider
          whether the decision
          can wait.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some choices are easy
          to reverse. Others
          carry consequences
          that can't simply
          be undone.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give those decisions
          the attention
          they deserve.
        </p>

        <p>
          You won't always
          make the perfect choice,
          and you can't guarantee
          the outcome. You can
          make a reasonable decision
          based on what you
          know and what matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            TEN MINUTES MATTERS.
            TEN MONTHS MATTERS.
            TEN YEARS MATTERS.
            FUTURE YOU GETS A VOTE.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
